    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d113">

                <pb facs="0223" n="189" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg189"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/5–252</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for Far
                        Eastern Affairs (Allison) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-05-02">May 2, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Reply to President Rhee’s Letter of March 21, 1952 to the
                        President.</item>
                </list>
                <p>I refer to your telephone conversation of May 2<anchor n="1" xml:id="d113-f1"/>
                    with the White House Press Secretary, Mr. Joseph Short, concerning a reply to
                    President Syngman Rhee’s letter of March 21, 1952. The following is the text of
                    a suggested informal message which Ambassador Muccio at Pusan might give to
                    President Rhee with regard to this letter:<anchor n="2" xml:id="d113-f2"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“You are requested to convey to Dr. Rhee the President’s greetings and
                            say that the President deeply appreciated his letter of March 21, 1952,
                            which he has read with great interest. You should then inform President
                            Rhee that the contents of his letter have been made available to the
                            various officials of the United States Government, concerned with Korean
                            affairs, and that the points which he has raised therein will be most
                            helpful in future policy considerations with respect to Korea.”<anchor
                                n="3" xml:id="d113-f3"/></p></quote></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d113-f1" n="1" xml:id="d113-f1-content">
                <p>See the memorandum, <ref target="#pg187">p. 187</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d113-f2" n="2" xml:id="d113-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 800 to Pusan, May 6, 1952, Muccio received instructions to convey
                    this informal message from Truman to Rhee. (795.00/5–652)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d113-f3" n="3" xml:id="d113-f3-content">
                <p>At the bottom of the source text are the initials “H.S.T.” in Truman’s
                    handwriting.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
